3. Why Did You Move From EC2 to EKS?
This is a very likely interview question.

Good answer:
“The main drivers were scalability, availability, operational efficiency, and containerization.

With EC2-based deployment, we had to manage the underlying servers and application processes more directly. With EKS, Kubernetes handles container scheduling, health checks, restarts, scaling, and service discovery.

It also gives us a standardized platform for deploying containerized applications.”

Benefits you can mention
Container orchestration
Horizontal scaling
Self-healing
High availability
Rolling deployments
Better resource utilization
Standardized deployment model
Easier application lifecycle management
Be careful with “cost reduction”
You mentioned operational cost reduction.

If asked:

“How exactly did EKS reduce cost?”

Don't automatically say that EKS itself is cheaper than EC2.

Say:

“The cost improvement was primarily from reducing operational overhead and improving resource utilization through containerization and orchestration. The actual infrastructure cost depends on workload and EKS architecture, so I wouldn't claim that EKS is inherently cheaper than EC2.”

That's a much stronger technical answer.

