57. A Strong 2–3 Minute Interview Answer
If they say:

“Explain your DOT-Portal CI/CD project end-to-end.”

Use this:

“For DOT-Portal, we modernized both the infrastructure and CI/CD architecture. Previously, the application was running on EC2 and deployments were managed through Jenkins. We moved the application to EKS and migrated the CI process to GitLab CI.

The application consists of UI and backend components, and we containerized both. For the Angular UI, we use a multi-stage Docker build. The first stage uses Node.js to install dependencies and build the Angular application, and the second stage uses Nginx as the runtime image and copies only the compiled application into it. For the backend, we use a Node slim image with the required dependencies and run the Node application using PM2.

In GitLab CI, we first perform SonarQube analysis for the UI and backend. We then build the Docker images and scan both images using Trivy for known vulnerabilities. The scan results are stored as pipeline artifacts. After successful validation, the images are pushed to our private Nexus Docker registry using a dedicated technical account.

For the release process, GitLab is integrated with XL-Release using a webhook. When the configured GitLab event occurs, XL-Release triggers the appropriate release template. XL-Release acts as the release orchestrator, while XL-Deploy handles the actual application deployment.

In XL-Deploy, we configured the EKS Kubernetes infrastructure, deployment environment, application, deployment package, and Helm chart. The deployment package contains the Helm chart and its configuration, and XL-Deploy deploys it to the target EKS cluster.

So the complete flow is GitLab, GitLab CI, SonarQube, Docker build, Trivy scan, Nexus, XL-Release, XL-Deploy, Helm, and finally EKS.

The main benefits were reducing manual deployment effort, improving consistency and traceability, and moving the application to a more scalable and resilient container orchestration platform.”

